      6: {
        a: "Make a comparison between Direct Tax and Indirect Tax.",
        b: "Discuss the benefits of GST in respect to business and industry, Central and State Governments and the Consumers.",
        answers: {
          a: "Direct Tax vs Indirect Tax:\n- Direct Tax is levied directly on the income or wealth of individuals or organizations (e.g., Income Tax, Corporate Tax). Indirect Tax is levied on goods and services and is collected by intermediaries (e.g., GST, VAT).\n- Direct Tax is progressive in nature, meaning the tax rate increases with higher income. Indirect Tax is generally regressive, as it takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income individuals.\n- Direct Tax cannot be shifted to another person; the taxpayer bears the burden. Indirect Tax can be shifted to the consumer, as it is included in the price of goods and services.\n- Direct Taxes are more transparent and easier to administer, while Indirect Taxes can be complex due to multiple rates and exemptions.",
          b: "Benefits of GST:\n- For Business and Industry: Simplifies the tax structure by replacing multiple taxes with a single tax, reduces compliance costs, and eliminates the cascading effect of taxes.\n- For Central and State Governments: Increases tax revenue through a broader tax base, improves efficiency in tax collection, and enhances transparency in the taxation system.\n- For Consumers: Reduces the overall cost of goods and services due to the elimination of cascading taxes, provides a uniform tax rate across states, and ensures better quality of goods and services due to increased competition.",
        },
      },
